=== 1.4 Conventions <conventions>

All varieties, stacks, and families are over $bb(C)$, and Chow groups have rational coefficients.
  On a smooth space we identify Chow classes with operational classes when pulling them to a test space.
  Products of stacks are understood in rational Chow theory [30] Theorem 2.1.12.
  We use proper rational pushforward for relative Deligne–Mumford maps and its compatibilities from [4]
  Theorem B.17 and Propositions B.18–B.19. Smooth Deligne–Mumford stacks have the operational identification in
  [4] Theorem C.6 and Corollary C.9; for smooth quotient stacks, see also [13] Propositions 4 and 19.
  Every formal series calculation is truncated at a specified coefficient, so only finitely many terms occur.

Parity is distinct from Chow codimension. In a super tensor product,
  interchanging homogeneous factors of parities $p \, q$ gives $\( - 1 \)^(p q)$.
  In particular, on an odd vector space the super-symmetric tensors are ordinary alternating tensors.
  All finite averaging and polarization arguments take place in characteristic zero.

